  "agreement_pct": 8.691311763437634,
  "pass_25pct": true,
  "note": "The two solvers approach this loop from opposite sides, and the mechanism is different at each end.  A uniform FDTD mesh reads low until its cells are inside the board's 0.40 mm clearances -- there are no nets in FDTD, only metal, so a cell that spans a clearance joins VBUS to GND -- and it climbs from 0.89 nH at 0.55 mm to 2.27 nH at 0.20 mm as that stops happening.  Snapping the mesh to the copper reaches the same place without the uniform cost: 2.27 nH, moving 2.2 % on the last refinement.  FastHenry falls down its own series, 4.56 nH at 1.8 mm to 2.49 nH at 0.5 mm, because a filament grid that coarse cannot let the return current concentrate under a leg running 0.4 mm above it, which is most of what sets this loop, and it is still falling 7 % per refinement.  The figure quoted for FastHenry here is the finest point of that sweep (it moved 7 % on its last step), 0.5 mm at 1 MHz, not the production run's 1.2 mm grid; the production model's own frequency sweep falls about 3 % between 1 MHz and 100 MHz, which is the band the FDTD extraction sits in, so the comparison is fair to within a few per cent either way.  Neither solver had been run to its own convergence before this.",
